1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Funding for this program is provided by

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Additional funding provided by

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Today we turn to John Locke.

4
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,500
On the face of it, Locke

5
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,000
is a powerful ally of the libertarian.

6
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
First, he believes, as libertarians today maintain,

7
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,000
that there are certain fundamental individual rights

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
that are not just for the libertarian.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
They are so important that no government,

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,000
even a representative government,

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
even a democratically elected government,

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,000
can override them.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,500
Not only that, he believes

14
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:19,000
that those fundamental rights include

15
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:28,000
a natural right to life, liberty, and property.

16
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
And, furthermore,

17
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
he argues that the right to property

18
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
is not just the creation of government or of law.

19
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
The right to property is a natural right

20
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,000
in the sense that it is pre-political.

21
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,500
It is a right that

22
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:58,000
attaches to individuals as human beings.

23
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Even before,

24
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
even before government comes on the scene,

25
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
even before parliaments and legislatures enact laws

26
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
to define rights and to enforce them.

27
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Locke says in order to think about

28
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
what it means to have a natural right,

29
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
we have to imagine the way things are

30
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
before government, before law.

31
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
And that's what Locke means by the state of nature.

32
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
He says the state of

33
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,000
nature is a state of liberty.

34
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Human beings are free and equal beings.

35
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
There is no natural hierarchy.

36
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:47,500
It's not the case that some

37
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:49,000
people are born to be kings

38
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
and others are born to be serfs.

39
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
We are free and equal in the state of nature.

40
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
And yet,

41
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
he makes the point that there's a difference

42
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:09,000
between a state of liberty and a state of license.

43
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,000
And the reason is that

44
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:11,000
even in the state of nature,

45
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:12,000
there is a kind of law.

46
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
It's not the kind of law that legislatures enact.

47
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
It's a law of nature.

48
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,000
And this law of nature constrains what we can do

49
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
even though we're free,

50
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
even though we're in the state of nature.

51
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Well, what are the constraints?

52
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
The only constraint given by the law of nature

53
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
is that the rights we have,

54
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
the natural rights we have,

55
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
we can't give up.

56
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Nor can we take them from somebody else.

57
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Under the law of nature,

58
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
I'm not free to take somebody else's life

59
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
or liberty or property.

60
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Nor am I free to take my own life

61
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
or liberty or property.

62
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Even though I'm free,

63
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
I'm not free to violate the law of nature.

64
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
I'm not free to take my own life

65
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
or to sell myself into slavery

66
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
or to give to somebody else

67
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:28,000
arbitrary absolute power over me.

68
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
So where does this constraint,

69
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
you may think it's a fairly minimal constraint,

70
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
but where does it come from?

71
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Well, Locke tells us where it comes from.

72
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
And he gives two answers.

73
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Here's the first answer.

74
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
For men,

75
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
being all the workmanship

76
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,000
of one omnipotent and infinitely wise maker,

77
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
namely God,

78
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
they are his property.

79
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Whose workmanship they are

80
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
made to last during his,

81
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
not one another's, pleasure.

82
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,000
So one answer to the question is

83
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
why can't I give up my natural rights

84
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
to life, liberty, and property is,

85
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:18,000
well, they're not strictly speaking yours.

86
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
After all, you are the creature of God.

87
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,000
God has a bigger property right in us.

88
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,000
A prior property right.

89
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Now you might say that's

90
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
an unsatisfying, unconvincing answer,

91
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
at least for those who don't believe in God.

92
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
What did Locke have to say to them?

93
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Well, here's where Locke appeals to the idea

94
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:47,000
of reason.

95
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,000
And this is the idea

96
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
that if we properly reflect

97
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
on what it means to be free,

98
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
we will be led to the conclusion

99
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
that freedom can't just be

100
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
a matter of doing whatever we want.

101
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
I think this is what Locke means

102
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
when he says,

103
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
the state of nature has a law of nature

104
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
to govern it which obliges everyone.

105
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
And reason, which is that law,

106
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
teaches mankind who will but consult it

107
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
that all being equal and independent,

108
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
no one ought to harm another in his life,

109
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,000
health, liberty, or possessions.

110
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
This leads to a point

111
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
where Locke's idea of

112
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,000
reason is a puzzling, paradoxical feature

113
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
of Locke's account of rights.

114
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Familiar in one sense,

115
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,000
but strange in another.

116
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
It's the idea that our natural rights

117
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
are unalienable.

118
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
What does unalienable mean?

119
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,000
It's not for us to alienate them

120
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,000
or to give them up,

121
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,000
to give them away,

122
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:52,000
to trade them away,

123
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
to sell them.

124
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Consider an airline ticket.

125
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Or tickets to the Patriots

126
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
or to the Red Sox.

127
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Non-transferable tickets

128
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
are unalienable.

129
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
I own them in the limited sense

130
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
that I can use them for myself,

131
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
but I can't trade them away.

132
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:14,000
So in one sense,

133
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
an unalienable right,

134
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
a non-transferable right,

135
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
makes something I own

136
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
less fully mine.

137
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
But in another sense

138
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,000
of unalienable rights,

139
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
especially where we're thinking

140
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
about life, liberty, and property,

141
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:39,000
for a right to be unalienable

142
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,000
makes it more deeply,

143
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:42,000
more profoundly mine.

144
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,000
And that's Locke's sense

145
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
of unalienable.

146
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
We see it in the American Declaration

147
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:50,000
of Independence.

148
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Thomas Jefferson drew on this idea

149
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:53,000
of Locke.

150
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
Unalienable rights,

151
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
to life, liberty,

152
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
and as Jefferson amended Locke,

153
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
to the pursuit of happiness.

154
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Unalienable rights.

155
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
Rights that are so

156
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
essentially mine

157
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,000
that even I can't trade them away

158
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:17,000
or give them up.

159
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,000
So these are the rights we have

160
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
in the state of nature

161
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
before there is any government.

162
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
In the case of life and property,

163
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
I can't take my own life,

164
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
I can't sell myself into slavery

165
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:30,000
any more than I can take

166
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,000
somebody else's life

167
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:32,000
or take someone else

168
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,000
as a slave by force.

169
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:37,000
But how does that work

170
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
in the case of property?

171
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Because it's essential

172
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
to Locke's case

173
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
that private property

174
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,000
can arise

175
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
even before there is any government.

176
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
How can there be a right

177
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
to private property

178
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:59,000
even before there is any government?

179
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
Locke's famous answer

180
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
comes in section 27.

181
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
Every man has a property

182
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:08,000
in his own person.

183
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:09,000
This nobody has any right to

184
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,000
but himself.

185
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:13,000
The labor of his body

186
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
and the work of his hands,

187
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:16,000
we may say,

188
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
are properly his.

189
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
So he moves

190
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,000
as the libertarians later

191
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
would move

192
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:26,000
from the idea

193
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
that we own ourselves,

194
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:30,000
that we have property

195
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
in our persons,

196
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:33,000
to the closely connected idea

197
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:36,000
that we own our own labor.

198
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:37,000
And from that

199
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
to the further claim

200
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
that whatever we mix

201
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
our labor with

202
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
that is unowned

203
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:49,000
becomes our property.

204
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Whatever he removes out of the state

205
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:51,000
that nature has provided

206
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
and left it in,

207
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
he has mixed his labor with

208
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:56,000
and joined it to something

209
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
that is his own

210
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
and thereby makes it his property.

211
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Why?

212
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
Because the labor

213
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
is the unquestionable property

214
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:09,000
of the laborer

215
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
and therefore no one

216
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,000
but the laborer

217
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:14,000
can have a right

218
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
to what is joined to

219
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
or mixed with

220
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
his labor.

221
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
And then he adds this important provision,

222
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,000
at least where there is enough

223
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
and is good left in common

224
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
for others.

225
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
But we not only

226
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
acquire a property

227
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
in the fruits of the earth,

228
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
in the deer that we hunt,

229
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
in the fish that we catch,

230
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
but also

231
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
if we till and plow

232
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:45,000
and enclose the land

233
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
and grow potatoes,

234
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
we own not only the potatoes

235
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:51,000
but the land,

236
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
the earth.

237
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
As much land

238
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:56,000
as a man tills,

239
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:57,000
plants, improves,

240
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:58,000
cultivates,

241
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
and can use,

242
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,000
the product of

243
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
so much is his property.

244
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
He by his labor

245
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:06,000
encloses it

246
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
from the commons.

247
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
So,

248
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,000
the idea that rights are unalienable

249
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,000
seems to distance Locke

250
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
from the libertarian.

251
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
The libertarian wants to say

252
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
we have an absolute property

253
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
right in ourselves

254
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
and therefore we can do with ourselves

255
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,000
whatever we want.

256
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Locke is not a sturdy ally

257
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
for that view.

258
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,000
In fact, he says if you take

259
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
natural rights seriously,

260
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:35,000
you'll be led to the idea

261
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
that there are certain constraints

262
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,000
on what we can do

263
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,000
with our natural rights,

264
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
constraints given

265
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
either by God

266
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:44,000
or by reason,

267
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:45,000
reflecting on what it means

268
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:46,000
really to be free.

269
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:50,000
And really to be free

270
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
means recognizing

271
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
that our rights are unalienable.

272
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:55,000
So here's the difference

273
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,000
between Locke and the libertarians,

274
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
but when it comes

275
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:59,000
to Locke's account

276
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
of private property,

277
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
he begins to look again

278
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,000
like a pretty good ally.

279
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:07,000
Because his argument

280
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,000
for private property

281
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,000
begins with the idea

282
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,000
that we are the proprietors

283
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
of our own person

284
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
and therefore of our labor,

285
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,000
and therefore of the fruits

286
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:16,000
of our labor,

287
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
including not only the things

288
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
we get,

289
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
which we gather

290
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:24,000
and hunt

291
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
in the state of nature,

292
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:28,000
but also we acquire

293
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:29,000
a property right

294
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:30,000
in the land that we enclose

295
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,000
and cultivate and improve.

296
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:35,000
There are some examples

297
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:36,000
that can bring out

298
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:40,000
the moral intuition

299
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
that our labor

300
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:43,000
can take something

301
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
that is unowned

302
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:48,000
and make it ours.

303
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:49,000
Though sometimes there are disputes

304
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:50,000
about this.

305
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:55,000
There's a debate

306
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
among rich countries

307
00:12:58,000 --> 00:12:59,000
and developing countries

308
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
about trade-related

309
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
intellectual property rights.

310
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
It came to a head recently

311
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
over drug patent laws.

312
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:09,000
Western countries,

313
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
and especially the United States,

314
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:12,000
say we have a big

315
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:13,000
pharmaceutical industry

316
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
that develops new drugs.

317
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
We want all countries

318
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
in the world to agree

319
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,000
to respect the patents.

320
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:25,000
Then there came along

321
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:26,000
the AIDS crisis

322
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
in South Africa.

323
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:33,000
And the American AIDS drugs

324
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
were hugely expensive,

325
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
far more than could be afforded

326
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
by most Africans.

327
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:41,000
So the South African government

328
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
said we are going to begin

329
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
to buy a generic version

330
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
of the AIDS antiretroviral

331
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:52,000
drug at a tiny fraction

332
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:53,000
of the cost

333
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:54,000
because we can find

334
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:57,000
an Indian manufacturing company

335
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:58,000
that figures out

336
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
how the thing is made

337
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
and produces it.

338
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:04,000
And for a tiny fraction

339
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:05,000
of the cost,

340
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,000
we can save lives

341
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
if we don't respect that patent.

342
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
And then the American government

343
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
said, no, here's a company

344
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:16,000
that invested research

345
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:17,000
and created this.

346
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:18,000
Right?

347
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:19,000
And they said,

348
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:20,000
we're going to buy

349
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:21,000
a generic drug.

350
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:22,000
You can't just start

351
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
mass producing these drugs

352
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
without paying a licensing fee.

353
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
So there was a dispute.

354
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
And the US,

355
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:32,000
the pharmaceutical companies

356
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
sued the South African government

357
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
to try to prevent their buying

358
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
the cheap, generic,

359
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:39,000
as they saw it,

360
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:44,000
pirated version of an AIDS drug.

361
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:45,000
And eventually,

362
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
the pharmaceutical industry

363
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
gave in and said,

364
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:50,000
all right, you can do that.

365
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:51,000
But this dispute

366
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
about what the rules

367
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
of property should be,

368
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
of intellectual property,

369
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
of drug patenting,

370
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:01,000
in a way,

371
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:02,000
is the last frontier

372
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
of the state of nature

373
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:05,000
because among nations

374
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
where there is no uniform law

375
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:09,000
of patent rights

376
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
and property rights,

377
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:12,000
it's up for grabs

378
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,000
until, by some act of consent,

379
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
some international agreement

380
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,000
that people enter into

381
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:26,000
some settled rules.

382
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,000
What about Locke's account

383
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
of private property

384
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:32,000
and how it can arise

385
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
before government

386
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
and before law comes on the scene?

387
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:40,000
Is it successful?

388
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:41,000
How many think

389
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
it's pretty persuasive?

390
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,000
Raise your hand.

391
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:50,000
How many don't find it persuasive?

392
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:51,000
All right, let's hear

393
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
for some critics.

394
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
What is wrong with Locke's account

395
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:57,000
of how private property

396
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
can arise without consent?

397
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
Yes.

398
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:03,000
Yeah.

399
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
I think it justifies

400
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:06,000
European cultural norms

401
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:07,000
as far as,

402
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:08,000
when you look at

403
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:09,000
how Native Americans

404
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:10,000
may not have cultivated

405
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:11,000
American land,

406
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
but by their arrival

407
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
in the Americas,

408
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
that contributed

409
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:18,000
to the development of America,

410
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:19,000
which wouldn't have otherwise

411
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:20,000
necessarily happened then

412
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,000
or by that specific group.

413
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:24,000
So you think

414
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:25,000
that this is a defense,

415
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
this defense of private property

416
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:28,000
in land?

417
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:29,000
Yes, because it complicates

418
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
original acquisition

419
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:32,000
if you only cite

420
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
the arrival of foreigners

421
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:35,000
that cultivated the land.

422
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:36,000
I see.

423
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:37,000
And what's your name?

424
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:38,000
Rochelle.

425
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:39,000
Rochelle?

426
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:40,000
Yeah.

427
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:41,000
Rochelle says,

428
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,000
this account of how property arises

429
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:47,000
would fit what was going on

430
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
in North America

431
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:55,000
during the time of the European settlement.

432
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:58,000
Do you think, Rochelle,

433
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
that it's a way of defending

434
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,000
the appropriation of the land?

435
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
Indeed, because he's also

436
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:10,000
justifying the Glorious Revolution,

437
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:11,000
so I don't think it's inconceivable

438
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:14,000
that he's also justifying colonization.

439
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:17,000
Well, that's an interesting

440
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
historical suggestion,

441
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:20,000
and I think there's a lot

442
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
to be said for it.

443
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
What do you think of the validity

444
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
of his argument, though?

445
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Because if you're right

446
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:29,000
that this would justify

447
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:32,000
the taking of land in North America

448
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
from Native Americans

449
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,000
who didn't enclose it,

450
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
if it's a good argument,

451
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:40,000
then Locke's given us

452
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:41,000
a justification for that.

453
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
If it's a bad argument,

454
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:46,000
then Locke's given us

455
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
a mere rationalization

456
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:52,000
that isn't morally defensible.

457
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
I'm leaning to the second one.

458
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:55,000
You're leaning toward the second.

459
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
But that's my opinion as well.

460
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:58,000
All right, well then,

461
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:02,000
let's hear if there's a defender

462
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:05,000
of Locke's account of private property,

463
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,000
and it would be interesting

464
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:10,000
if they could address Rochelle's worry

465
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:11,000
that this is just a way

466
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
of defending the,

467
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:14,000
of appropriation of land

468
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
by the American colonists

469
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
from the Native Americans

470
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:19,000
who didn't enclose it.

471
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
Is there someone who will defend Locke

472
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
on that point?

473
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:28,000
You ready?

474
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:29,000
Are you going to defend Locke?

475
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
You're accusing him of justifying

476
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
the European, basically, massacre

477
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:34,000
of the Native Americans.

478
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
But who says he's defending it?

479
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,000
Maybe the European colonization isn't right.

480
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
You know, maybe it's the state of war

481
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:42,000
that he talked about

482
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:45,000
in his second treatise, you know?

483
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,000
So the wars between the Native Americans

484
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:53,000
and the colonists, the settlers,

485
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:56,000
that might have been the state of war

486
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
that we can only emerge from

487
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:01,000
by an agreement or an act of consent,

488
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
and that's what would have been required

489
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:04,000
fairly to resolve it.

490
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:05,000
Yeah, and both sides would

491
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:06,000
have had to agree to it

492
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
and carry it out and everything.

493
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
But what about, and what's your name?

494
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:11,000
Dan.

495
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:14,000
Dan, what about, Rochelle says,

496
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:19,000
this argument in Section 27 and then in 32

497
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:23,000
about appropriating land,

498
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
that argument, if it's valid,

499
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:29,000
would justify the settlers appropriating that land

500
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:32,000
and excluding others from it.

501
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
Do you think that argument's a good argument?

502
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
Well, doesn't it kind of imply

503
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,000
that the Native Americans hadn't already done that?

504
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:44,000
Well, the Native Americans, as hunter-gatherers,

505
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:47,000
didn't actually enclose, enclose land.

506
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,000
So I think Rochelle is onto something there.

507
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
What I want to, I'd like to...

508
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:54,000
Go ahead, Dan.

509
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
At the same time, he's saying that

510
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
just by picking an acorn or taking an apple

511
00:19:58,000 --> 00:19:59,500
or maybe killing a buffalo

512
00:19:59,500 --> 00:20:01,000
on a certain amount of land,

513
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
that makes it yours because it's your labor

514
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
and that's, you know, your labor

515
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:06,000
would enclose that land.

516
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
So by that definition,

517
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
maybe they didn't have fences around,

518
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
around little plots of land but didn't...

519
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:13,000
They were using it.

520
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
Yeah, by Locke's definition.

521
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:16,000
So maybe by Locke's definition,

522
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,000
the Native Americans could have claimed their property right

523
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:20,000
in the land itself.

524
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:23,000
But they just didn't have Locke on their side,

525
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:24,000
as she points out.

526
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
Okay, that's good.

527
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
One more defender of Locke.

528
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
Go ahead.

529
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
Well, I mean, just to defend Locke,

530
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
he does say that there are some,

531
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:37,000
sometimes in which you can't take another person's land.

532
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
For example, you can't acquire land

533
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:40,000
that,

534
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:41,000
that is common property.

535
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:42,000
See, people in,

536
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:43,000
in terms of American Indians,

537
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
I feel like they already have civilizations themselves

538
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
and they were using land in common.

539
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:48,000
So it's kind of like what,

540
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
an analogy to what he was talking about with like,

541
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
the common English property.

542
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:55,000
You can't take land that everybody's sharing in common.

543
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:56,000
Oh, that's interesting.

544
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:57,000
And also you can't,

545
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:00,000
you can't take land unless you make sure that there's

546
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:01,500
as much land as possible left

547
00:21:01,500 --> 00:21:03,000
for other people to take as well.

548
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
So if you're taking common,

549
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:06,500
so you have to make

550
00:21:06,500 --> 00:21:08,000
sure that whenever you take land

551
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:09,000
or that there's enough land,

552
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
that there's enough left for other people to use.

553
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
That's just as good as the land that you took.

554
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:14,000
So.

555
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:15,000
That's, it's, it's true.

556
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
Locke says there has to be this

557
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
right to private property in the earth

558
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:22,000
is subject to the provision that there be as much

559
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
and as good left for others.

560
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:25,000
What's your name?

561
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:26,000
Right.

562
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:27,000
I'm Fang.

563
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
So Fang in a way agrees with Dan

564
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
that maybe there is a claim within Locke's framework

565
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:35,000
that could be developed on behalf of the Native Americans.

566
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:39,000
Here's the further question.

567
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:45,000
If the right to private property is natural,

568
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
not conventional,

569
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:50,000
if it's something that we acquire

570
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:53,000
even before we agree to government,

571
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,000
how does that right constrain

572
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:59,000
what a legitimate government can do?

573
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:03,000
In order for us to be able to do that,

574
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:08,000
in order finally to see whether is,

575
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:13,000
whether Locke is an ally or potentially a critic

576
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:17,000
of the libertarian idea of the state,

577
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:20,000
we have to ask what becomes of our natural rights

578
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:23,000
once we enter into society.

579
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:24,500
We know that the way we

580
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:26,000
enter into society is by consent,

581
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
by agreement.

582
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
To leave the state of nature

583
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
and to be governed by the majority.

584
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:33,000
And by consent.

585
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:34,000
And by a system of laws.

586
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
Human laws.

587
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:40,000
But those human laws

588
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,000
are only legitimate

589
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:47,000
if they respect our natural rights.

590
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:50,000
If they respect our unalienable rights

591
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:54,000
to life, liberty, and property.

592
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:57,000
No parliament,

593
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
no legislature,

594
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:02,000
however democratic its credentials,

595
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:09,000
can legitimately violate our natural rights.

596
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:12,000
This idea

597
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:15,000
that no law can violate our right

598
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:18,000
to life, liberty, and property would seem

599
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:24,000
to support the idea of a government so limited

600
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,500
that it would gladden the

601
00:23:26,500 --> 00:23:29,000
heart of the libertarian after all.

602
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
But

603
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:35,000
those hearts should not be so quickly gladdened.

604
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
Because even though

605
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
for Locke,

606
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
the law of nature persists

607
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:44,000
once government arrives,

608
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:48,000
even though Locke insists on limited government,

609
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:52,000
government limited by the end for which it was created,

610
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
namely the preservation of property,

611
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:55,000
even so,

612
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,000
there's an important sense

613
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:00,000
in which what counts as my property

614
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:07,000
what counts as respecting my life and liberty

615
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:12,000
are for the government to define.

616
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:17,000
That there be property,

617
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:19,000
that there be respect

618
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:22,000
for life and liberty

619
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:26,000
is what limits government.

620
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
But what counts

621
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:33,000
as respecting my life

622
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:36,000
and respecting my property,

623
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:39,000
that is for governments

624
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:42,000
to decide and to define.

625
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:45,000
How can that be?

626
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:48,000
Is Locke contradicting himself?

627
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:52,000
Or is there an important distinction here?

628
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:55,000
In order to answer that question,

629
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
which will decide Locke's fit with the law,

630
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:00,000
Locke's fit with the libertarian view,

631
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
we need to look closely

632
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:05,000
at what legitimate government looks like for Locke,

633
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
and we turn to that next time.

634
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:18,000
Nikolai, if you didn't think you'd get caught,

635
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:21,000
would you pay your taxes?

636
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:25,000
Um, I don't think so.

637
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:26,000
I would,

638
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:29,000
I would rather have a system, personally,

639
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,000
that I would, that I could give money

640
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:34,000
to exactly those sections of the government

641
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:35,000
that I support,

642
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:38,000
and not just blanket support everything.

643
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
You'd rather be in the state of nature,

644
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
at least on April 15th.

645
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:52,000
Last time,

646
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
we began

647
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:57,000
to discuss Locke's state of nature,

648
00:25:57,000 --> 00:26:00,000
his account of private property,

649
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:05,000
his theory of legitimate government,

650
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
which is government based on consent

651
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:10,000
and also limited government.

652
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:14,000
Locke believes in certain fundamental rights

653
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:17,000
that constrain what government can do,

654
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:21,000
and he believes that those rights are natural rights,

655
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,000
not rights that flow from law.

656
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
Or from government.

657
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:28,000
And so Locke's great

658
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:32,000
philosophical experiment is to see

659
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
if he can give an account

660
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
of how there could be

661
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
a right to private property

662
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
without consent

663
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:43,000
before government

664
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,000
and legislators arrive on the scene

665
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:47,000
to define property.

666
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,000
That's his question.

667
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:50,000
That's his claim.

668
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
There is a way, Locke argues,

669
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
to create property,

670
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:57,000
not just in the things we gather and hunt,

671
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
but in the land itself.

672
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,000
Provided there is enough

673
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:04,000
and is good luck for others.

674
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:07,000
Today I want to turn to the question

675
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:08,000
of consent,

676
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:11,000
which is Locke's second big idea.

677
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:13,000
Private property is one,

678
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:15,000
consent is the other.

679
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:21,000
What is the work of consent?

680
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:25,000
People here have been invoking

681
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:28,000
the idea of consent since we began,

682
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
since the first week.

683
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
You remember when we were talking about

684
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
pushing the fat man off the bridge,

685
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:35,000
someone said,

686
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:38,000
but he didn't agree to sacrifice himself.

687
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:41,000
It would be different if he consented.

688
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:45,000
Or when we were talking about the cabin boy,

689
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:48,000
killing and eating the cabin boy.

690
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:49,000
Some people said,

691
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:50,000
well,

692
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
if they had consented to a lottery,

693
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:53,000
it would be different,

694
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:55,000
then it would be all right.

695
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:57,000
So consent has come up a lot,

696
00:27:57,000 --> 00:27:59,000
and here in John Locke,

697
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:04,000
we have one of the great philosophers of consent.

698
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:08,000
Consent is an obvious, familiar idea

699
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
in moral and political philosophy.

700
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:13,000
Locke says that legitimate government

701
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:15,000
is government founded on consent,

702
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:18,000
and who nowadays would disagree with him?

703
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:22,000
Sometimes when the ideas of political philosophers

704
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:27,000
are as familiar as Locke's ideas about consent,

705
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:29,000
it's hard to make sense of them,

706
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:32,000
or at least to find them very interesting.

707
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,000
But there are some puzzles,

708
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
some strange features

709
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:39,000
of Locke's account of consent

710
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:42,000
as the basis of legitimate government,

711
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:45,000
and that's what I'd like to take up today.

712
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:47,000
One way to look at it,

713
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:51,000
one way of testing

714
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:54,000
the plausibility of Locke's idea of consent,

715
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:59,000
and also of probing some of its perplexities,

716
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:04,000
is to ask just what a legitimate government

717
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:07,000
founded on consent can do,

718
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:11,000
what are its powers according to Locke?

719
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:14,000
Well, in order to answer that question,

720
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:17,000
it helps to,

721
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:20,000
remember what the state of nature is like.

722
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:22,000
Remember, the state of nature

723
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:26,000
is the condition that we decide to leave,

724
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:30,000
and that's what gives rise to consent.

725
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,000
Why not stay there?

726
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:35,000
Why bother with government at all?

727
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:38,000
Well, what's Locke's answer to that question?

728
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:41,000
He says there are some inconveniences

729
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:42,000
in the state of nature,

730
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:45,000
but what are those inconveniences?

731
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:46,000
The main inconvenience

732
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:50,000
is that everyone

733
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:54,000
can enforce the law of nature.

734
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:56,000
Everyone is an enforcer,

735
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:59,000
or what Locke calls the executor

736
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:02,000
of the state of nature.

737
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:06,000
And he means executor literally.

738
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:10,000
If someone violates the law of nature,

739
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:13,000
he's an aggressor,

740
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:16,000
he's beyond reason,

741
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:20,000
and you can punish him.

742
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:23,000
And you don't have to be too careful or fine

743
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:25,000
about gradations of punishment

744
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:26,000
in the state of nature.

745
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:28,000
You can kill him.

746
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:29,000
You can certainly kill someone

747
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:33,000
who comes after you,

748
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:35,000
tries to murder you.

749
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:37,000
That's self-defense.

750
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:38,000
But the enforcement power,

751
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:39,000
the right to punish,

752
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:41,000
everyone can do the punishing

753
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:43,000
in the state of nature.

754
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:45,000
And not only can you punish with death

755
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:48,000
people who come after you,

756
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:51,000
seeking to take your life,

757
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:53,000
you can also punish a thief

758
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:55,000
who tries to steal your goods,

759
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:58,000
because that also counts as aggression

760
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:01,000
against the law of nature.

761
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:03,000
If someone has stolen

762
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:05,000
from a third party,

763
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:07,000
you can go after him.

764
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:09,000
Why is this?

765
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:11,000
Well, violations of the law of nature

766
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:14,000
are an act of aggression.

767
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:15,000
There is no police force,

768
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:16,000
there are no judges,

769
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:18,000
no juries.

770
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:20,000
So everyone is the judge

771
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:24,000
in his or her own case.

772
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:26,000
And Locke observes that

773
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:27,000
when people are the judges

774
00:31:27,000 --> 00:31:28,000
of their own cases,

775
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:32,000
they tend to get carried away.

776
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:35,000
And this gives rise to the inconvenience

777
00:31:35,000 --> 00:31:38,000
in the state of nature.

778
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:40,000
People overshoot the mark.

779
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:41,000
There's aggression.

780
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:44,000
There's punishment.

781
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:45,000
And before you know it,

782
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:47,000
everybody is insecure

783
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:50,000
in the enjoyment

784
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:52,000
of his or her unalienable rights

785
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:56,000
to life, liberty, and property.

786
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:58,000
Now he describes in pretty harsh

787
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:02,000
and even grim terms

788
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:04,000
what you can do to people

789
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:07,000
who violate the law of nature.

790
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:09,000
One may destroy a man

791
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:11,000
who makes war upon him

792
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:12,000
for the same reason

793
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:14,000
that he may kill a wolf

794
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:16,000
or a lion.

795
00:32:16,000 --> 00:32:18,000
Such men have no other rule

796
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:20,000
but that of force and violence.

797
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:21,000
Listen to this.

798
00:32:21,000 --> 00:32:23,000
And so may be treated

799
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:25,000
as beasts of prey

800
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:29,000
those dangerous and noxious creatures

801
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:31,000
that will be sure to destroy you

802
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:33,000
if you fall into their power.

803
00:32:33,000 --> 00:32:36,000
So kill them first.

804
00:32:36,000 --> 00:32:39,000
So what starts out

805
00:32:39,000 --> 00:32:42,000
as a seemingly benign

806
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:44,000
state of nature

807
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:45,000
where everyone's free

808
00:32:45,000 --> 00:32:47,000
and yet where there is a law

809
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:49,000
and the law respects people's rights

810
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:51,000
and those rights are so powerful

811
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:52,000
that they're unalienable,

812
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:54,000
what starts out

813
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:57,000
looking very benign

814
00:32:57,000 --> 00:32:59,000
once you look closer

815
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:01,000
is pretty fierce

816
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:03,000
and filled with violence

817
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:07,000
and that's why people want to leave.

818
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:09,000
How do they leave?

819
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:12,000
Well, here's where consent comes in.

820
00:33:12,000 --> 00:33:14,000
The only way to escape

821
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:16,000
from the state of nature

822
00:33:16,000 --> 00:33:20,000
is to undertake

823
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:23,000
an act of consent

824
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,000
where you agree

825
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:29,000
to give up the enforcement power

826
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:32,000
and to create a government

827
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:34,000
or a community

828
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:36,000
where there will be

829
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:38,000
a legislature

830
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:40,000
to make law

831
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:42,000
and where everyone

832
00:33:42,000 --> 00:33:44,000
agrees in advance,

833
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:46,000
everyone who enters,

834
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:47,000
agrees in advance

835
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:49,000
to abide by whatever

836
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:51,000
the majority decides.

837
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:52,000
But then the question,

838
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:53,000
and this is our question

839
00:33:53,000 --> 00:33:54,000
and here's where I want

840
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:55,000
to get your views,

841
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:56,000
then the question is

842
00:33:56,000 --> 00:33:59,000
what powers,

843
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:02,000
what can the majority decide?

844
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:04,000
Now here it gets tricky

845
00:34:04,000 --> 00:34:06,000
for Locke

846
00:34:06,000 --> 00:34:07,000
because you remember

847
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:08,000
alongside the whole story

848
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:09,000
about consent

849
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:11,000
and majority rule

850
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:14,000
there are these natural rights,

851
00:34:14,000 --> 00:34:15,000
the law of nature,

852
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:17,000
these unalienable rights

853
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:18,000
and you remember

854
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:20,000
they don't disappear

855
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:22,000
when people

856
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:23,000
join together

857
00:34:23,000 --> 00:34:26,000
to create a civil society.

858
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:29,000
So even once the majority is in charge

859
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:32,000
the majority can't violate

860
00:34:32,000 --> 00:34:36,000
your inalienable rights,

861
00:34:36,000 --> 00:34:38,000
can't violate your fundamental right

862
00:34:38,000 --> 00:34:40,000
to life, liberty and property.

863
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:45,000
So here's the puzzle.

864
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:46,000
How much power

865
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:48,000
does the majority have?

866
00:34:48,000 --> 00:34:50,000
How limited is the government

867
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:55,000
created by consent?

868
00:34:55,000 --> 00:34:57,000
It's limited by

869
00:34:57,000 --> 00:35:00,000
the obligation

870
00:35:00,000 --> 00:35:01,000
on the part of the majority

871
00:35:01,000 --> 00:35:02,000
to respect

872
00:35:02,000 --> 00:35:06,000
and to enforce

873
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:08,000
the fundamental natural rights

874
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:09,000
of the citizens.

875
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:10,000
They don't give those up,

876
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:11,000
we don't give those up

877
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:13,000
when we enter government.

878
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:14,000
That's this powerful idea

879
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:17,000
taken over from Locke

880
00:35:17,000 --> 00:35:18,000
by Jefferson

881
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:22,000
in the Declaration.

882
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:24,000
Unalienable rights.

883
00:35:24,000 --> 00:35:27,000
So let's go to our two cases.

884
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:28,000
Remember Michael Jordan,

885
00:35:28,000 --> 00:35:29,000
Bill Gates,

886
00:35:29,000 --> 00:35:31,000
the libertarian objection

887
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:33,000
to taxation for redistribution.

888
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:34,000
Well what about

889
00:35:34,000 --> 00:35:36,000
Locke's limited government?

890
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:37,000
Is there anyone who thinks

891
00:35:37,000 --> 00:35:40,000
that Locke

892
00:35:40,000 --> 00:35:43,000
does give grounds

893
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:46,000
for opposing taxation

894
00:35:46,000 --> 00:35:50,000
for redistribution?

895
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:52,000
Anybody?

896
00:35:52,000 --> 00:35:54,000
Go ahead.

897
00:35:54,000 --> 00:35:55,000
If you,

898
00:35:55,000 --> 00:35:56,000
if the majority rules

899
00:35:56,000 --> 00:35:58,000
that there should be taxation,

900
00:35:58,000 --> 00:36:00,000
even if the minority

901
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:02,000
should still not have to be taxed

902
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:04,000
because that's taking away property

903
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:08,000
which is one of the rights of nature.

904
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:10,000
Alright, so,

905
00:36:10,000 --> 00:36:12,000
and what's your name?

906
00:36:12,000 --> 00:36:13,000
Ben.

907
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:14,000
Ben.

908
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:15,000
So,

909
00:36:15,000 --> 00:36:17,000
if the majority taxed,

910
00:36:17,000 --> 00:36:22,000
taxes the minority,

911
00:36:22,000 --> 00:36:24,000
without the consent of the minority

912
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:27,000
to that particular tax law,

913
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:29,000
it does amount to a taking

914
00:36:29,000 --> 00:36:30,000
of their property

915
00:36:30,000 --> 00:36:32,000
without their consent

916
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:34,000
and it would seem that

917
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:37,000
Locke should object to that.

918
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:39,000
You're not going to object to that?

919
00:36:39,000 --> 00:36:41,000
Do you want some textual support

920
00:36:41,000 --> 00:36:42,000
for your view,

921
00:36:42,000 --> 00:36:44,000
for your reading of Locke, Ben?

922
00:36:44,000 --> 00:36:45,000
Alright.

923
00:36:45,000 --> 00:36:47,000
I brought some along

924
00:36:47,000 --> 00:36:49,000
just in case you raised it.

925
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:53,000
If you've got,

926
00:36:53,000 --> 00:36:54,000
if you have your texts,

927
00:36:54,000 --> 00:36:55,000
look at 138,

928
00:36:55,000 --> 00:36:57,000
passage 138.

929
00:36:57,000 --> 00:36:59,000
The supreme power,

930
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:01,000
by which Locke means the legislature,

931
00:37:01,000 --> 00:37:02,000
cannot take from any man

932
00:37:02,000 --> 00:37:04,000
any part of his property

933
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:06,000
without his own consent.

934
00:37:06,000 --> 00:37:08,000
For the preservation of property

935
00:37:08,000 --> 00:37:10,000
being the end of government,

936
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:12,000
and that for which men enter into society,

937
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:15,000
it necessarily supposes and requires

938
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:17,000
that people should have property.

939
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:18,000
That was the whole reason

940
00:37:18,000 --> 00:37:20,000
for entering society in the first place,

941
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:21,000
to protect the right to property.

942
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:23,000
And when Locke speaks about

943
00:37:23,000 --> 00:37:24,000
the right to property,

944
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:26,000
he often uses that

945
00:37:26,000 --> 00:37:28,000
as a kind of global term

946
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:30,000
for the whole category,

947
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:32,000
the right to life, liberty, and property.

948
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:37,000
So, that part of Locke

949
00:37:37,000 --> 00:37:39,000
at beginning of 138

950
00:37:39,000 --> 00:37:42,000
seems to support Ben's reading.

951
00:37:42,000 --> 00:37:45,000
But what about the part of 138

952
00:37:45,000 --> 00:37:48,000
if you keep reading?

953
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:50,000
Men, therefore, in society,

954
00:37:50,000 --> 00:37:52,000
having property,

955
00:37:52,000 --> 00:37:54,000
they have such a right to the goods

956
00:37:54,000 --> 00:37:57,000
which by the law of the community

957
00:37:57,000 --> 00:37:59,000
are theirs.

958
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:01,000
Look at this.

959
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:03,000
And that no one can take from them

960
00:38:03,000 --> 00:38:05,000
without their consent.

961
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:07,000
And then at the end,

962
00:38:07,000 --> 00:38:09,000
of this passage, he says,

963
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:11,000
so it's a mistake to think

964
00:38:11,000 --> 00:38:12,000
that the legislative power

965
00:38:12,000 --> 00:38:13,000
can do what it will

966
00:38:13,000 --> 00:38:15,000
and dispose of the estates

967
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:17,000
of the subject arbitrarily

968
00:38:17,000 --> 00:38:19,000
or take any part of them at pleasure.

969
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:22,000
Here's what's elusive.

970
00:38:24,000 --> 00:38:26,000
On the one hand, he says,

971
00:38:26,000 --> 00:38:28,000
the government can't take your property

972
00:38:28,000 --> 00:38:29,000
without your consent.

973
00:38:29,000 --> 00:38:30,000
He's clear about that.

974
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:33,000
But then he goes on to say,

975
00:38:33,000 --> 00:38:35,000
I mean, that's the natural right to property.

976
00:38:35,000 --> 00:38:37,000
But then it seems to me

977
00:38:37,000 --> 00:38:39,000
that property, what counts as property,

978
00:38:39,000 --> 00:38:41,000
is not natural but conventional,

979
00:38:41,000 --> 00:38:43,000
defined by the government.

980
00:38:45,000 --> 00:38:47,000
The goods which by the law

981
00:38:47,000 --> 00:38:50,000
of the community are theirs.

982
00:38:52,000 --> 00:38:53,000
And the plot thickens

983
00:38:53,000 --> 00:38:57,000
if you look ahead to section 140.

984
00:38:57,000 --> 00:38:59,000
In 140, he says,

985
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:00,000
governments can't be supported

986
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:01,000
without great charge.

987
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:03,000
Government is expensive.

988
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:05,000
And it's fit that everyone

989
00:39:05,000 --> 00:39:06,000
who enjoys his share of the protection

990
00:39:06,000 --> 00:39:08,000
should pay out of his estate.

991
00:39:10,000 --> 00:39:12,000
And then here's the crucial line.

992
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:15,000
But still it must be with his own consent,

993
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:18,000
i.e. the consent of the majority,

994
00:39:19,000 --> 00:39:20,000
giving it either by themselves

995
00:39:20,000 --> 00:39:22,000
or through their representatives.

996
00:39:22,000 --> 00:39:25,000
So what is Locke actually saying?

997
00:39:27,000 --> 00:39:30,000
Property is natural in one sense,

998
00:39:30,000 --> 00:39:32,000
but conventional in another.

999
00:39:32,000 --> 00:39:34,000
It's natural in the sense that

1000
00:39:34,000 --> 00:39:35,000
we have a fundamental right,

1001
00:39:35,000 --> 00:39:38,000
we have a fundamental, unalienable right,

1002
00:39:38,000 --> 00:39:40,000
that there be property,

1003
00:39:40,000 --> 00:39:42,000
that the institution of property

1004
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:45,000
exist and be respected by the government.

1005
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:48,000
So an arbitrary taking of property

1006
00:39:48,000 --> 00:39:52,000
would be a violation of the law of nature

1007
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:55,000
and would be illegitimate.

1008
00:39:55,000 --> 00:39:57,000
But it's a further question,

1009
00:39:57,000 --> 00:40:00,000
here's the conventional aspect of property,

1010
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:01,000
it's a further question,

1011
00:40:01,000 --> 00:40:04,000
what counts as property,

1012
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:06,000
how it's defined,

1013
00:40:06,000 --> 00:40:09,000
and what counts as taking property,

1014
00:40:09,000 --> 00:40:12,000
and that's up to the government.

1015
00:40:12,000 --> 00:40:14,000
So the consent,

1016
00:40:14,000 --> 00:40:18,000
here we're coming back to our question,

1017
00:40:18,000 --> 00:40:21,000
what is the work of consent?

1018
00:40:21,000 --> 00:40:24,000
What it takes for taxation to be legitimate

1019
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:27,000
is that it be by consent,

1020
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:30,000
not the consent of Bill Gates himself

1021
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:32,000
if he's the one who has to pay the tax,

1022
00:40:32,000 --> 00:40:36,000
but by the consent that he and we,

1023
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:38,000
all of us within the society gave

1024
00:40:38,000 --> 00:40:40,000
when we emerged from the state of nature

1025
00:40:40,000 --> 00:40:42,000
and created the government

1026
00:40:42,000 --> 00:40:45,000
in the first place.

1027
00:40:45,000 --> 00:40:49,000
It's the collective consent.

1028
00:40:49,000 --> 00:40:53,000
And by that reading,

1029
00:40:53,000 --> 00:40:58,000
it looks like consent is doing a whole lot

1030
00:40:58,000 --> 00:41:01,000
and the limited government consent creates

1031
00:41:01,000 --> 00:41:06,000
isn't all that limited.

1032
00:41:06,000 --> 00:41:08,000
Does anyone want to respond to that

1033
00:41:08,000 --> 00:41:11,000
or have a question about that?

1034
00:41:11,000 --> 00:41:13,000
Go ahead.

1035
00:41:13,000 --> 00:41:15,000
Stand up.

1036
00:41:15,000 --> 00:41:18,000
Well, I'm just wondering what Locke's view is

1037
00:41:18,000 --> 00:41:22,000
on once you have a government that's already in place,

1038
00:41:22,000 --> 00:41:25,000
whether it is possible for people

1039
00:41:25,000 --> 00:41:27,000
who are born into that government

1040
00:41:27,000 --> 00:41:30,000
to then leave and return to the state of nature.

1041
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:32,000
I mean, I don't think that

1042
00:41:32,000 --> 00:41:34,000
Locke mentioned that at all in the...

1043
00:41:34,000 --> 00:41:36,000
What do you think?

1044
00:41:36,000 --> 00:41:40,000
Well, I think as the convention,

1045
00:41:40,000 --> 00:41:44,000
it would be very difficult to leave the government

1046
00:41:44,000 --> 00:41:49,000
because you are no longer...

1047
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:50,500
because nobody else is just

1048
00:41:50,500 --> 00:41:52,000
living in the state of nature.

1049
00:41:52,000 --> 00:41:57,000
Everybody else is now governed by this legislature.

1050
00:41:57,000 --> 00:41:59,000
What would it mean today, you're asking,

1051
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:00,000
and what's the answer?

1052
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:01,000
What's your name?

1053
00:42:01,000 --> 00:42:02,000
Nicola.

1054
00:42:02,000 --> 00:42:03,000
Nicola.

1055
00:42:03,000 --> 00:42:04,000
To leave the state...

1056
00:42:04,000 --> 00:42:07,000
Suppose you wanted to leave civil society today.

1057
00:42:07,000 --> 00:42:09,000
You want to withdraw your consent

1058
00:42:09,000 --> 00:42:11,000
and return to the state of nature.

1059
00:42:11,000 --> 00:42:13,000
Well, because you didn't actually consent to it.

1060
00:42:13,000 --> 00:42:14,000
You were just born into it.

1061
00:42:14,000 --> 00:42:18,000
It was your ancestors who joined.

1062
00:42:18,000 --> 00:42:19,000
Right.

1063
00:42:19,000 --> 00:42:20,000
So...

1064
00:42:20,000 --> 00:42:23,000
You didn't sign the social contract.

1065
00:42:23,000 --> 00:42:24,000
I didn't sign it.

1066
00:42:24,000 --> 00:42:25,000
Exactly.

1067
00:42:25,000 --> 00:42:26,000
All right.

1068
00:42:26,000 --> 00:42:28,000
So what does Locke say there?

1069
00:42:28,000 --> 00:42:29,000
Yeah.

1070
00:42:29,000 --> 00:42:31,000
I don't think Locke says

1071
00:42:31,000 --> 00:42:33,000
that you have to sign anything.

1072
00:42:33,000 --> 00:42:34,000
I think that he says

1073
00:42:34,000 --> 00:42:35,000
that it's kind of implied consent.

1074
00:42:35,000 --> 00:42:37,000
And by leaving and taking government services,

1075
00:42:37,000 --> 00:42:39,000
you are implying that you are consenting

1076
00:42:39,000 --> 00:42:41,000
to the government taking things from you.

1077
00:42:41,000 --> 00:42:42,000
All right.

1078
00:42:42,000 --> 00:42:43,000
So implied consent,

1079
00:42:43,000 --> 00:42:45,000
that's a partial answer to this challenge.

1080
00:42:45,000 --> 00:42:47,000
Now, you may not think that implied consent

1081
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:49,000
is as good as the real thing.

1082
00:42:49,000 --> 00:42:51,000
Is that what you're shaking your head about, Nicola?

1083
00:42:51,000 --> 00:42:52,000
Speak up.

1084
00:42:52,000 --> 00:42:54,000
Stand up and speak up.

1085
00:42:54,000 --> 00:42:56,000
I don't think that necessarily

1086
00:42:56,000 --> 00:42:58,000
just by utilizing the government's...

1087
00:42:58,000 --> 00:43:05,000
you know, various resources that we are necessarily implying

1088
00:43:05,000 --> 00:43:07,500
that we agree with the

1089
00:43:07,500 --> 00:43:10,000
way that this government was formed.

1090
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:12,500
Or that we have consented to

1091
00:43:12,500 --> 00:43:15,000
actually join into the social contract.

1092
00:43:15,000 --> 00:43:18,000
So you don't think the idea of implied consent

1093
00:43:18,000 --> 00:43:21,000
is strong enough to generate any obligation at all

1094
00:43:21,000 --> 00:43:23,000
to obey the government?

1095
00:43:23,000 --> 00:43:25,000
Not necessarily, no.

1096
00:43:25,000 --> 00:43:28,000
Nicola, if you didn't think you'd get caught,

1097
00:43:28,000 --> 00:43:31,000
would you pay your taxes?

1098
00:43:31,000 --> 00:43:33,000
Um...

1099
00:43:33,000 --> 00:43:35,000
I don't think so.

1100
00:43:35,000 --> 00:43:39,000
I would rather have a system, personally,

1101
00:43:39,000 --> 00:43:42,000
that I could give money to

1102
00:43:42,000 --> 00:43:45,000
exactly those sections of the government

1103
00:43:45,000 --> 00:43:46,000
that I support,

1104
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:49,000
and not just blanket support everything.

1105
00:43:49,000 --> 00:43:51,000
You'd rather be in the state of nature,

1106
00:43:51,000 --> 00:43:53,000
at least on April 15th.

1107
00:43:57,000 --> 00:43:58,000
But, Nicola,

1108
00:43:58,000 --> 00:44:00,000
what I'm trying to get at is,

1109
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:02,000
do you consider that you're under no obligation

1110
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:05,000
since you haven't actually entered into any act of consent,

1111
00:44:05,000 --> 00:44:07,000
but for prudential reasons,

1112
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:08,000
you do what you're supposed

1113
00:44:08,000 --> 00:44:09,000
to do according to the law?

1114
00:44:09,000 --> 00:44:11,000
Exactly.

1115
00:44:11,000 --> 00:44:13,000
If you look at it that way,

1116
00:44:13,000 --> 00:44:15,000
then you're violating another one of Locke's treatises,

1117
00:44:15,000 --> 00:44:18,000
which is that you can't take anything from anyone else.

1118
00:44:18,000 --> 00:44:22,000
Like, you can't take the government services

1119
00:44:22,000 --> 00:44:24,000
and then not give them anything in return.

1120
00:44:24,000 --> 00:44:25,500
If you want to go

1121
00:44:25,500 --> 00:44:27,000
live in the state of nature,

1122
00:44:27,000 --> 00:44:28,000
that's fine,

1123
00:44:28,000 --> 00:44:30,000
but you can't take anything for the government

1124
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:31,000
because by the government's terms,

1125
00:44:31,000 --> 00:44:32,500
which are the only terms under

1126
00:44:32,500 --> 00:44:34,000
which you can enter the agreement,

1127
00:44:34,000 --> 00:44:35,000
say that you have to

1128
00:44:35,000 --> 00:44:36,000
pay taxes to take those things.

1129
00:44:36,000 --> 00:44:38,000
So you're saying that Nicola can

1130
00:44:38,000 --> 00:44:40,000
go back into the state of nature

1131
00:44:40,000 --> 00:44:41,000
if she wants to,

1132
00:44:41,000 --> 00:44:43,000
but she can't drive on Mass Ave.

1133
00:44:43,000 --> 00:44:44,000
Exactly.

1134
00:44:44,000 --> 00:44:48,000
I want to raise the stakes beyond using Mass Ave.

1135
00:44:48,000 --> 00:44:49,000
Yes.

1136
00:44:49,000 --> 00:44:51,000
And even beyond taxation.

1137
00:44:51,000 --> 00:44:53,000
What about life?

1138
00:44:53,000 --> 00:44:55,000
What about military conscription?

1139
00:44:55,000 --> 00:44:56,000
Yes.

1140
00:44:56,000 --> 00:44:58,000
What do you say? Stand up.

1141
00:44:58,000 --> 00:45:00,000
First of all, we have to

1142
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:02,000
remember that sending people to war

1143
00:45:02,000 --> 00:45:05,000
is not necessarily implying that they'll die.

1144
00:45:05,000 --> 00:45:08,000
I mean, obviously, you're not raising their chances here,

1145
00:45:08,000 --> 00:45:11,000
but it's not a death penalty.

1146
00:45:11,000 --> 00:45:13,000
So if you're going to

1147
00:45:13,000 --> 00:45:15,000
discuss whether or not military conscription

1148
00:45:15,000 --> 00:45:19,000
is equivalent to suppressing people's right to life,

1149
00:45:19,000 --> 00:45:21,000
you shouldn't approach it that way.

1150
00:45:21,000 --> 00:45:25,000
Secondly, the real problem here is Locke has this view

1151
00:45:25,000 --> 00:45:28,000
about consent and natural rights.

1152
00:45:28,000 --> 00:45:30,000
But you're not allowed to

1153
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:32,000
give up your natural rights either.

1154
00:45:32,000 --> 00:45:34,500
So the real question is, you know,

1155
00:45:34,500 --> 00:45:37,000
how does he himself figure it out

1156
00:45:37,000 --> 00:45:39,500
between I agree to give up

1157
00:45:39,500 --> 00:45:42,000
my life, give up my property,

1158
00:45:42,000 --> 00:45:44,000
when he talks about taxes

1159
00:45:44,000 --> 00:45:46,000
or military conscription for the fact,

1160
00:45:46,000 --> 00:45:49,000
but I guess Locke would be against suicide.

1161
00:45:49,000 --> 00:45:51,000
And that's still, you know, my own consent.

1162
00:45:51,000 --> 00:45:53,000
I agree to taking my life.

1163
00:45:53,000 --> 00:45:54,000
Eric.

1164
00:45:54,000 --> 00:45:56,000
So Eric brings us back to

1165
00:45:56,000 --> 00:45:58,000
the puzzle we've been wrestling with

1166
00:45:58,000 --> 00:46:00,000
since we started reading Locke.

1167
00:46:00,000 --> 00:46:04,000
On the one hand, we have these unalienable rights

1168
00:46:04,000 --> 00:46:06,000
to life, liberty, and property,

1169
00:46:06,000 --> 00:46:08,000
which means that even we don't

1170
00:46:08,000 --> 00:46:10,000
have the power to give them up.

1171
00:46:10,000 --> 00:46:14,000
And that's what creates the limits on legitimate government.

1172
00:46:14,000 --> 00:46:17,000
It's not what we consent to that limits government.

1173
00:46:17,000 --> 00:46:20,000
It's what we lack the power

1174
00:46:20,000 --> 00:46:23,000
to give away when we consent.

1175
00:46:24,000 --> 00:46:31,000
That's the point at the heart of Locke's whole account

1176
00:46:31,000 --> 00:46:34,000
of legitimate government.

1177
00:46:34,000 --> 00:46:36,500
But now you say, well, if

1178
00:46:36,500 --> 00:46:39,000
we can't give up our own life,

1179
00:46:39,000 --> 00:46:41,000
if we can't commit suicide,

1180
00:46:41,000 --> 00:46:43,000
if we can't give up our right to property,

1181
00:46:43,000 --> 00:46:44,500
how can we then agree

1182
00:46:44,500 --> 00:46:46,000
to be bound by a majority

1183
00:46:46,000 --> 00:46:49,000
that will force us to sacrifice

1184
00:46:49,000 --> 00:46:52,000
our lives or give up our property?

1185
00:46:52,000 --> 00:46:54,000
Is Locke at all convinced?

1186
00:46:54,000 --> 00:46:56,000
Does Locke have a way out of this?

1187
00:46:56,000 --> 00:47:04,000
Or is he basically sanctioning an all-powerful government,

1188
00:47:04,000 --> 00:47:09,000
despite everything he says about unalienable rights?

1189
00:47:09,000 --> 00:47:11,000
Does he have a way out of it?

1190
00:47:11,000 --> 00:47:13,000
Who would speak here in defense of Locke

1191
00:47:13,000 --> 00:47:16,000
or make sense, find a way out of this predicament?

1192
00:47:16,000 --> 00:47:17,000
All right, go ahead.

1193
00:47:17,000 --> 00:47:19,000
I feel like there's a general distinction to be made

1194
00:47:19,000 --> 00:47:22,000
between the right to life that individuals possess

1195
00:47:22,000 --> 00:47:24,000
and the fact that a government

1196
00:47:24,000 --> 00:47:28,000
cannot take away a single individual's right to life.

1197
00:47:28,000 --> 00:47:31,000
I think if you look at conscription as the government

1198
00:47:31,000 --> 00:47:34,000
picking out certain individuals to go fight in war,

1199
00:47:34,000 --> 00:47:36,000
then that would be a violation

1200
00:47:36,000 --> 00:47:38,000
of their natural right to life.

1201
00:47:38,000 --> 00:47:41,000
On the other hand, if you have conscription,

1202
00:47:41,000 --> 00:47:43,000
let's say a lottery, for example,

1203
00:47:43,000 --> 00:47:45,500
then in that case, I

1204
00:47:45,500 --> 00:47:48,000
would view that as the population

1205
00:47:48,000 --> 00:47:49,500
picking their representatives to defend

1206
00:47:49,500 --> 00:47:51,000
them in the case of war.

1207
00:47:51,000 --> 00:47:52,500
The idea being that since the

1208
00:47:52,500 --> 00:47:54,000
whole population cannot go out there

1209
00:47:54,000 --> 00:47:56,000
to defend its own right to property,

1210
00:47:56,000 --> 00:47:58,000
it picks its own representatives through a process

1211
00:47:58,000 --> 00:48:00,000
that's essentially random.

1212
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:03,000
And then these sort of elected representatives

1213
00:48:03,000 --> 00:48:06,000
go out and fight for the rights of the people.

1214
00:48:06,000 --> 00:48:08,000
It works very similar,

1215
00:48:08,000 --> 00:48:11,000
it works just like an elected government, in my opinion.

1216
00:48:11,000 --> 00:48:14,000
All right, so an elected government can conscript citizens

1217
00:48:14,000 --> 00:48:17,000
to go out and defend the way of life,

1218
00:48:17,000 --> 00:48:22,000
the community that makes the enjoyment of rights possible?

1219
00:48:22,000 --> 00:48:23,000
I think so.

1220
00:48:23,000 --> 00:48:25,000
I think it can, because to

1221
00:48:25,000 --> 00:48:27,000
me it seems that it's very similar

1222
00:48:27,000 --> 00:48:31,000
to the process of electing representatives to a legislature.

1223
00:48:31,000 --> 00:48:38,000
Although here, it's as if the government is electing

1224
00:48:38,000 --> 00:48:42,000
by conscription certain citizens to go

1225
00:48:42,000 --> 00:48:46,000
die for the sake of the whole.

1226
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:48,500
Is that consistent with respect

1227
00:48:48,500 --> 00:48:51,000
for a natural right to liberty?

1228
00:48:51,000 --> 00:48:53,000
Well, what I would say there is,

1229
00:48:53,000 --> 00:48:56,000
there is a distinction between picking out individuals

1230
00:48:56,000 --> 00:49:01,000
and having a random choice of individuals.

1231
00:49:01,000 --> 00:49:03,000
Between picking out, let me make sure,

1232
00:49:03,000 --> 00:49:05,000
between picking out individuals.

1233
00:49:05,000 --> 00:49:06,000
Well, by...

1234
00:49:06,000 --> 00:49:07,000
Let me, what's your name?

1235
00:49:07,000 --> 00:49:08,000
Gokul.

1236
00:49:08,000 --> 00:49:10,000
Gokul says there's a

1237
00:49:10,000 --> 00:49:12,000
difference between picking out individuals

1238
00:49:12,000 --> 00:49:14,000
to lay down their lives,

1239
00:49:14,000 --> 00:49:17,000
and having a general law,

1240
00:49:17,000 --> 00:49:21,000
I think this is the answer Locke would give, actually.

1241
00:49:21,000 --> 00:49:23,000
Gokul.

1242
00:49:23,000 --> 00:49:25,000
Locke is against arbitrary government.

1243
00:49:25,000 --> 00:49:28,000
He's against the arbitrary taking.

1244
00:49:28,000 --> 00:49:30,000
The singling out of Bill Gates

1245
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:32,000
to finance the war in Iraq.

1246
00:49:32,000 --> 00:49:35,000
He's against singling out a particular citizen,

1247
00:49:35,000 --> 00:49:37,000
or group of people,

1248
00:49:37,000 --> 00:49:39,000
to go off and fight.

1249
00:49:39,000 --> 00:49:42,000
But if there's a general law,

1250
00:49:42,000 --> 00:49:45,000
such that the government's choice,

1251
00:49:45,000 --> 00:49:49,000
the majority's action is non-arbitrary,

1252
00:49:49,000 --> 00:49:52,000
it doesn't really amount to a violation

1253
00:49:52,000 --> 00:49:58,000
of people's basic rights.

1254
00:49:58,000 --> 00:50:00,000
What does count as a violation

1255
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:02,000
is an arbitrary taking,

1256
00:50:02,000 --> 00:50:04,000
because that would essentially say,

1257
00:50:04,000 --> 00:50:06,000
not only to Bill Gates, but to everyone,

1258
00:50:06,000 --> 00:50:08,000
there is no rule of law.

1259
00:50:08,000 --> 00:50:11,000
There is no institution of property.

1260
00:50:11,000 --> 00:50:14,000
Because at the whim of the king,

1261
00:50:14,000 --> 00:50:16,000
or for that matter, of the parliament,

1262
00:50:16,000 --> 00:50:20,000
we can name you, or you,

1263
00:50:20,000 --> 00:50:22,000
to give up your rights,

1264
00:50:22,000 --> 00:50:23,000
or your property,

1265
00:50:23,000 --> 00:50:25,000
or to give up your life.

1266
00:50:25,000 --> 00:50:27,500
But so long as there

1267
00:50:27,500 --> 00:50:30,000
is a non-arbitrary rule of law,

1268
00:50:30,000 --> 00:50:33,000
then,

1269
00:50:33,000 --> 00:50:35,000
it's permissible.

1270
00:50:35,000 --> 00:50:37,000
Now you may say this doesn't amount

1271
00:50:37,000 --> 00:50:40,000
to a very limited government.

1272
00:50:40,000 --> 00:50:42,000
And the libertarian may complain

1273
00:50:42,000 --> 00:50:46,000
that Locke is not such a terrific ally after all.

1274
00:50:46,000 --> 00:50:48,000
The libertarian has two grounds

1275
00:50:48,000 --> 00:50:50,000
for disappointment in Locke.

1276
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:52,000
First,

1277
00:50:52,000 --> 00:50:55,000
that the rights are unalienable,

1278
00:50:55,000 --> 00:50:58,000
and therefore I don't really own myself after all.

1279
00:50:58,000 --> 00:51:00,000
I can't dispose of my life,

1280
00:51:00,000 --> 00:51:02,000
or my liberty, or my property,

1281
00:51:02,000 --> 00:51:05,000
in a way that violates my rights.

1282
00:51:05,000 --> 00:51:07,000
That's disappointment number one.

1283
00:51:07,000 --> 00:51:09,000
Disappointment number two,

1284
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:11,000
once there is a legitimate government

1285
00:51:11,000 --> 00:51:13,000
based on consent,

1286
00:51:13,000 --> 00:51:16,000
the only limits for Locke

1287
00:51:16,000 --> 00:51:20,000
are limits on arbitrary takings

1288
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:21,000
of life,

1289
00:51:21,000 --> 00:51:22,000
or of liberty,

1290
00:51:22,000 --> 00:51:24,000
or of property.

1291
00:51:24,000 --> 00:51:25,000
But if the majority decides,

1292
00:51:25,000 --> 00:51:27,000
if the majority promulgates

1293
00:51:27,000 --> 00:51:31,000
a generally applicable law,

1294
00:51:31,000 --> 00:51:33,000
and if it votes

1295
00:51:33,000 --> 00:51:36,000
duly, according to fair procedures,

1296
00:51:36,000 --> 00:51:39,000
then there is no violation.

1297
00:51:39,000 --> 00:51:41,000
Whether it's a system of taxation,

1298
00:51:41,000 --> 00:51:44,000
or a system of conscription.

1299
00:51:44,000 --> 00:51:47,000
So it's clear that Locke

1300
00:51:47,000 --> 00:51:50,000
is worried about the absolute arbitrary power

1301
00:51:50,000 --> 00:51:53,000
of kings.

1302
00:51:53,000 --> 00:51:55,000
But it's also true,

1303
00:51:55,000 --> 00:51:58,000
and here's the darker side of Locke,

1304
00:51:58,000 --> 00:52:00,000
that this great theorist of consent

1305
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:02,000
came up with a theory of private property

1306
00:52:02,000 --> 00:52:06,000
that didn't require consent.

1307
00:52:06,000 --> 00:52:08,000
That may,

1308
00:52:08,000 --> 00:52:09,500
and this goes back to

1309
00:52:09,500 --> 00:52:11,000
the point Rochelle made last time,

1310
00:52:11,000 --> 00:52:16,000
may have had something to do with Locke's second concern,

1311
00:52:16,000 --> 00:52:18,000
which was America.

1312
00:52:18,000 --> 00:52:19,000
You remember,

1313
00:52:19,000 --> 00:52:21,000
when he talks about the state of nature,

1314
00:52:21,000 --> 00:52:25,000
he's not talking about an imaginary place.

1315
00:52:25,000 --> 00:52:27,000
In the beginning, he says,

1316
00:52:27,000 --> 00:52:29,000
all the world was America.

1317
00:52:29,000 --> 00:52:31,000
And what was going on in America?

1318
00:52:31,000 --> 00:52:35,000
The settlers were enclosing land

1319
00:52:35,000 --> 00:52:40,000
and engaged in wars with the Native Americans.

1320
00:52:40,000 --> 00:52:43,000
Locke, who was an administrator

1321
00:52:43,000 --> 00:52:46,000
of one of the colonies,

1322
00:52:46,000 --> 00:52:48,000
may have been as interested

1323
00:52:48,000 --> 00:52:51,000
in providing a justification

1324
00:52:51,000 --> 00:52:53,000
for private property through enclosure

1325
00:52:53,000 --> 00:52:55,000
without consent,

1326
00:52:55,000 --> 00:52:57,000
through enclosure and cultivation,

1327
00:52:57,000 --> 00:53:01,000
as he was with developing a theory

1328
00:53:01,000 --> 00:53:04,000
of government based on consent

1329
00:53:04,000 --> 00:53:06,000
that would reign in kings

1330
00:53:06,000 --> 00:53:09,000
and arbitrary rulers.

1331
00:53:09,000 --> 00:53:11,000
The question we're left with,

1332
00:53:11,000 --> 00:53:14,000
the fundamental question we still haven't answered is,

1333
00:53:14,000 --> 00:53:16,000
what then becomes of consent?

1334
00:53:16,000 --> 00:53:18,000
What work can it do?

1335
00:53:18,000 --> 00:53:20,000
What is its moral force?

1336
00:53:20,000 --> 00:53:23,000
What are the limits of consent?

1337
00:53:23,000 --> 00:53:25,000
Consent matters not only for governments,

1338
00:53:25,000 --> 00:53:28,000
but also for markets.

1339
00:53:28,000 --> 00:53:30,000
And beginning next time,

1340
00:53:30,000 --> 00:53:32,000
we're going to take up questions

1341
00:53:32,000 --> 00:53:34,000
of the limits of consent

1342
00:53:34,000 --> 00:53:37,000
in the buying and selling of goods.

